# Line terminal

mm.terminal provides a fixed character grid for displaying a byte stream.
The caller supplies storage, chooses a font, and initializes the display.
The module uses mm.fonts for glyphs, mm.gfx for composition and RGB565 expansion,
and mm.display for output. It can be used independently of a console transport.

# Text handling

Printable ASCII writes one cell and wraps at the right edge. Wrap is deferred:
a newline immediately after a full line moves down only once. At the bottom,
advancing a line scrolls the grid up. LF moves to column zero on the next row;
CR moves to column zero on the current row. Backspace erases the preceding cell
without crossing a line boundary. Tab fills with spaces to the next multiple
of eight columns; form feed clears the grid and homes the cursor.

Bytes above ASCII become question marks. Other controls are ignored. This is
a line terminal; ANSI escape parsing, UTF-8 decoding, scrollback, and a painted
cursor are outside its current interface.

# Module API

Terminal::initialize(columns, rows, cells) validates dimensions and borrows
cells. Keep that storage alive while the terminal is used. write(string_view)
updates the grid; cell(column, row), column(), row(), and dirty() expose its
state. Calls to write before initialization return NotInitialized. A failed
initialization preserves the previous grid.

The default colors are amber-orange text (RGB 255, 176, 0) on black. Monochrome
displays use white text on black.

present(display, surface, font, scratch, force) renders the current grid into
a caller-owned one-bit Surface and refreshes the display. The font and complete
grid must fit the surface, which must fit the display. RGB565 output requires
scratch space of two bytes per pixel in one surface row. The module allocates
no storage. Successful presentation clears dirty; failed output remains dirty
for retry. force requests repaint even when the text has not changed.

# Tool on Linux

The build helper detects the native architecture and selects Clang and SDL:

```sh
./scripts/build-terminal.sh
```

Add --run to open the terminal after building, --compiler to choose a compiler,
or --dry-run to preview the commands. It restores the previous configuration
unless --keep is given. The executable is placed at
out-target-<triple>/tools/terminal/terminal.

Install the project's Linux and SDL dependencies first. From the project root,
configure a native SDL target, build the tool, then run its target executable.
For an aarch64 Linux host:

```sh
./configure --target aarch64-linux-gnu --target-host --compiler clang++ \
    --sdk linux-aarch64 --board sdl-linux-aarch64 --runner native --build debug
./build --target tools/terminal/
./out-target-aarch64-linux-gnu/terminal
```

On x86_64 Linux, use x86_64 instead of aarch64 in the target, SDK, board and
output directory. The target must match the development machine. The native
SDL target supplies both display and console providers; a plain host build
has no display provider.

The tool reads mm.stdio's selected console and shows the bytes in the display
window. The console's normal line discipline applies: type a line in the
launching terminal and press Enter. Ctrl-C ends the process. It does not start
a child shell or read keyboard input from the SDL window.

--once reads one available chunk (up to 256 bytes), renders it, and exits.
--help prints usage. Normal mode polls every 10 milliseconds. A zero-byte
successful read is treated as idle: mm.stdio has no separate EOF status.
The Linux provider reports pipe hangup as TransportError, which ends the tool
with an error; use redirected file input for a --once smoke check. Idle display
refreshes also service the SDL event queue.

# Tests

```sh
./test --host tests/mm/terminal/
```

The mm.test suite exercises wrapping, scrolling, carriage return, tabs,
backspace, clear, storage bounds, failed initialization, and display retry,
including RGB565 scratch validation. No physical display is required.
